# Supabase secrets and sensitive files
.env
.env.local
.env.production
supabase/.env
supabase/functions/.env
**/secrets.json
**/*secret*
!scripts/secret-scan.sh
**/*private*

# Supabase MCP specific
supabase-mcp-config.json
**/supabase-access-token*
**/supabase-service-key*

# Any files containing access tokens or API keys
**/*token*
**/*credential*
!public-key*
!*.public.key

# ...but not source files that merely have one of those words in the name.
#
# The four substring patterns above (*secret*, *private*, *token*, *credential*)
# match any path segment anywhere, source code included. That is not
# theoretical: supabase/migrations/..._make_customer_buckets_private.sql was
# silently dropped by *private*, so the commit meant to close the public-bucket
# exposure shipped with no migration in it, and the test it added in the same
# breath was red from that day on. `git add` on an ignored path is a no-op with
# a hint that scrolls past in a scripted commit.
#
# Secrets stay out by scanning CONTENT, not by guessing from filenames:
# scripts/secret-scan.sh runs on every commit via .husky/pre-commit. Key
# material is still ignored by extension - .key, .pem, .p12, .json, .env, .csv,
# .txt are deliberately NOT re-included below.
!**/*.ts
!**/*.tsx
!**/*.js
!**/*.jsx
!**/*.mjs
!**/*.cjs
!**/*.sql
!**/*.swift
!**/*.sh

